# AI Gateway Platform - Cursor Rules

## Project Context
Building a cost-effective AI Gateway with multi-tenancy support in Java Spring Boot.

## Code Standards
- Use Spring Boot 3.x with WebFlux (reactive programming)
- Follow standard Spring project structure
- Use constructor injection (not @Autowired field injection)
- Prefer records for DTOs (Java 17+)
- Use Lombok for reducing boilerplate

## Architecture Patterns
- Controller → Service → Client (adapter) pattern
- Controllers: Handle HTTP, validation, return reactive types
- Services: Business logic, rate limiting, cost calculation
- Clients: External API calls (OpenAI, Anthropic, etc.)

## Package Structure
- Base package: com.lppduy.aigateway

## Naming Conventions
- Controllers: `*Controller` (e.g., ChatController)
- Services: `*Service` (e.g., RateLimitService)
- Clients: `*Client` (e.g., OpenAIClient)
- DTOs: `*Request`, `*Response`
- Config: `*Config`

## Code Style
- Use meaningful variable names
- Add JavaDoc for public methods
- Include error handling for all external calls
- Log important operations (use SLF4J)
- Write defensive code with validation

## Testing
- Use JUnit 5 + Mockito
- Test services thoroughly
- Mock external API calls (use MockWebServer)
- Integration tests for critical flows

## When Generating Code
1. Follow reactive patterns (Mono/Flux)
2. Handle errors with proper HTTP status codes
3. Include request/response logging
4. Add input validation
5. Consider performance (non-blocking I/O)

## Common Pitfalls to Avoid
- Don't block in reactive code (no .block() in production)
- Don't forget to handle empty Mono/Flux
- Always set timeouts for external calls
- Don't expose internal errors to clients

